[{"id":868249,"t":"Rocket Fuel","a":"Gino Wickman","r":3.75,"d":"2026-10-18","p":208,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/18548215/","v":"Pretty interesting business book, definitely speaks to some of the org structure challenges we have at Readwise.\n\nThis book’s biggest plus is that it really just makes one big point (the Visionary/Integrator framework), and makes it well!\n\nBut not amazingly written or imo detailed enough to get into the 4+ star level.","c":["#ef7522","#efebec","#e1cdc1"],"ar":0.667},{"id":733685,"t":"Son of the Poison Rose","a":"Jonathan Maberry","r":4,"d":"2026-10-18","p":704,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/23230193/","v":"Not a bad book 2 in my opinion! Meandered a bit but still quite fun. \n\nStill very much John Wick But With Magic TM","c":["#23040b","#500821","#7d1136"],"ar":0.652},{"id":2318,"t":"Harry Potter and the Methods of Rationality","a":"Eliezer Yudkowsky","r":null,"d":"2026-10-15","p":null,"u":null},{"id":887523,"t":"Can't Hurt Me","a":"David Goggins","r":4,"d":"2026-10-14","p":364,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/17083197/","s":"Master Your Mind and Defy the Odds","c":["#100d0b","#bf9c54","#ecedf2"],"ar":0.648},{"id":887520,"t":"The Body Keeps the Score","a":"Bessel A. van der Kolk","r":3.25,"d":"2026-10-14","p":520,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16594142/","s":"Brain, Mind, and Body in the Healing of Trauma","c":["#4279bc","#4579bd","#4179bc"],"ar":0.65},{"id":2398,"t":"Mistborn","a":"Brandon Sanderson","r":null,"d":"2026-10-14","p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1963143/","s":"The Final Empire","c":["#211a20","#31242b","#79564d"],"ar":0.664},{"id":30797,"t":"High Output Management","a":"Andrew S. Grove","r":4.5,"d":"2026-09-29","p":272,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/2175377/","v":"This is a classic for a reason :)\n\nThe canonical silicon valley management book, that gets into the theory as well as lots of practical advice on 1on1s, measuring output, performance reviews, “task-relevant maturity”, etc. \n\nGrove’s writing style is so succinct and sooo clear. He will often drop bangers that don’t sound controversial at all, but upon reflection completely reframe how to manage. One such mindnumbingly simple (but revolutionary) idea, on how to know if a manager is doing a good job:\n\n“The output of a manager is the output of the organizational units under his or her supervision or influence.”\n\nHighly recommend!","c":["#253a82","#ed8c41","#d7cbca"],"ar":0.648},{"id":65453,"t":"Kagen the Damned","a":"Jonathan Maberry","r":3.75,"d":"2026-09-28","p":560,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16270245/","v":"Basically John Wick but with magic\n\nI love a good revenge arc… especially when stuff gets dark and this is dark.\n\nA lot of this was great (the plot, the idea of the characters, etc)… but something about the writing style or pacing felt off to me\n\nWill still be reading on to the next one!","c":["#284b64","#0c152b","#13274a"],"ar":0.662},{"id":478200,"t":"Bright Steel","a":"Miles Cameron","r":3.75,"d":"2026-09-05","p":464,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1328343/","v":"Man what is with this guy and abrupt series endings 😭\n\nReally unique fantasy trilogy, and I had no idea where it was going the entire time, engaging to read… but just really didnt feel that it came together amazingly at the end.","c":["#000000","#223e5c","#091019"],"ar":0.666},{"id":395233,"t":"Dark Forge","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4,"d":"2026-08-28","p":432,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1276084/","v":"I mean, im still gonna finish the trilogy at this point… overall well done but still just not hitting as much as i’m used to from Miles Cameron!","c":["#000000","#25130b","#050102"],"ar":0.667},{"id":365878,"t":"Cold Iron","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4,"d":"2026-08-22","p":496,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/3803489/","v":"Went into this book in my stupor of depression after finishing the author’s Traitor Son Cycle, hoping for more.\n\nThis series is very very different. Much more generic fantasy following a young protagonist discovering his world… not altogether bad but really didnt hit quite the same as the other series. I will still read on though…","c":["#000000","#121212","#bdbdbe"],"ar":0.667},{"id":292436,"t":"The Fall of Dragons","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4.5,"d":"2026-08-17","p":688,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16583579/","v":"What a fuckin ride dawg. This was the fifth and final book in the Traitor Son Cycle… and it delivered!\n\nI’d give the overall series 4.75. One of my favorite of all time. Quite challenging to keep track of everything/everyone, but the characters are fucking amazing if you can… and the battles are detailed incredibly (also quite brutal/dark… realistic perhaps).\n\nI’d say this specific book had a few weaknesses: it felt like it really ended too quickly, without tying up many many loose ends or character arcs (I dont need an end for every of the 100+ characters, but man that shit was abrupt), and stuff felt a little too “easy” in many respects.\n\nBut man the whole book was basically straight action, and satisfying in so many regards.","c":["#1d1b17","#839689","#b0c3b6"],"ar":0.669},{"id":135887,"t":"The Motive","a":"Patrick Lencioni","r":3.75,"d":"2026-08-17","p":192,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/21986530/","v":"Some great lessons in here, but too short imo and i would say only some of the lessons (and some of the protagonist’s arc) apply to me.\n\nBut definitely going to take away some valuable things from this book, around tough conversations and meetings :)\n\nWorth reading if you lead a team!","c":["#0a1113","#373b3f","#454748"],"ar":0.674},{"id":173623,"t":"The Plague of Swords","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4,"d":"2026-08-04","p":572,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19483441/","v":"Fourth book out of five in this series… this one felt (perhaps inevitably) like mostly just a setup for the final installment.\n\nStill great, still killed off some of my favorite characters chaotically (😭), still a fascinating universe, but yeah.. the ending felt more cliffhanger than anything.\n\nCan’t wait for the last book, but need to hold off until we ship some overdue projects at Readwise or I will simply not have any sleep 😂","c":["#1c1a16","#78613e","#3d3227"],"ar":0.668},{"id":135644,"t":"The Odyssey","a":"Homer","r":4.5,"d":"2026-07-25","p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/22066193/","v":"How do you even review a book like The Odyssey?\n\nIn terms of being an engaging read, I can’t say it captivated me like modern fantasy… But given a discount for its 2800 YEARS OLD age it was surprisingly engaging and I was actually quite locked in by the time Odysseus returned (spoiler alert).\n\nAnother way I like to evaluate fiction is by how it impacted me, changed the way I see the world, or what it taught me. With that lense, I would also give it high marks: it was illuminating to see where so many archetypes and “tropes” came from. And I think the story and characters and lessons resonated deeply enough that I’ll take their lessons into my everyday life. At the same time, I wouldn’t say the story “changed my life” (though maybe many other stories inspired by it did).\n\nAnother thing that I found fascinating is how alien of a culture (with different moralities: like yes you should obviously slaughter the suitors with no mercy) ancient greece was, and how much modern books take our modern ethics for granted.\n\nSo yeah. That’s how i got to a 4.5/5. Would recommend! Very excited to see the movie now 😎","c":["#eea51a","#030d1a","#7d5d44"],"ar":0.653},{"id":112252,"t":"The Dread Wyrm","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4.75,"d":"2026-07-10","p":608,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19467888/","v":"This series is just so fucking good. \n\nThe ending felt a little rushed, but still amazing.\n\nI need to take a break before reading the final two books in the series, because I end up staying up too late reading this and wrecking my sleep 😭","c":["#1c0f0a","#8e876f","#343023"],"ar":0.667},{"id":86802,"t":"The Fell Sword","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4.5,"d":"2026-06-21","p":640,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16305696/","v":"Yet another banger… i am loving this series\n\nSlightly worse than the first book, largely in this case just because some of the POVs were boring worldbuilding. \n\nBut man, still so good. Can’t believe i had never heard of this series.","c":["#0e0f10","#485669","#728ea8"],"ar":0.666},{"id":65404,"t":"The Red Knight","a":"Miles Cameron","r":4.75,"d":"2026-06-04","p":300,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16303487/","v":"Probably the best novel I read this year! \n\nSuper gritty, dark fantasy set in medieval times… with a cast of like 50+ characters. \n\nNgl when i first got thrown into this book it was quite challenging.  You kinda just have to accept you’re not going to remember a ton of names upfront (or know whats going on) and just roll with it… but it ends up so worth it. \n\nIncredible depth of characters, incredible detail of battles (wtf turns out i love siege warfare), and incredible universe. Can’t wait to read the next one.","c":["#010000","#291614","#b17748"],"ar":0.666},{"id":58374,"t":"The Strength of the Few","a":"James Islington","r":4,"d":"2026-05-09","p":736,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19849838/","v":"Some parts of this really dragged, and didnt feel super meaningful… but it was quite fun enough. I’m still gonna read the next one when it comes out!\n\n(And pls let Obiteum be over)","c":["#060712","#625f67","#41414c"],"ar":0.666},{"id":54677,"t":"The Will of the Many","a":"James Islington","r":4.5,"d":"2026-04-23","p":640,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19116106/","v":"Really fun!\n\nngl it was probably more of a 4/4.25 most of the book (i just dont vibe with the “academia” setting or Vis’s character that intensely) but MAN the last 10% got me and bumped it to a 4.5. I love not knowing what the fuck is going on.","c":["#282320","#6e5a4a","#382f29"],"ar":0.666},{"id":36840,"t":"Midnight Falcon","a":"David Gemmell","r":4.5,"d":"2026-04-07","p":448,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/23146416/","v":"Awesome continuation of the Rigante series. It takes place 20 years later and follows the last book’s protagonist’s bastard son (the LBPBS?)… which i was initially pretty annoyed by but he managed to win me over!\n\nThe pacing was a little weird… the final battle was actually pretty quick and almost felt like the book peaked 60% of the way through. Still super satisfying though! Loved how deep Gemmell went with the characters here and their internal struggles, quite different from his other books.","c":["#28130d","#7d4f2f","#492519"],"ar":0.603},{"id":32851,"t":"The E-Myth Revisited","a":"Michael E. Gerber","r":4.5,"d":"2026-04-04","p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/23438747/","v":"Great book on running a small business, the very common pitfalls that practitioners turned entrepreneurs (it me) fall into.\n\nI especially resonated with the model of the entrepreneur vs manager vs technician. \n\nProbably if I had read this a few years ago it wouldn’t have “hit” the same, but as Readwise has crossed 20 employees a loot of the issues described have become really apparent to me. It became the right book at the right time, I felt like the author was describing me every time he described things entrepreneurs fuck up 🙈\n\nBut not anymore 🚀🚀🚀","c":["#ffffff","#394975","#c0c2b7"],"ar":0.663},{"id":36841,"t":"Project Hail Mary","a":"Andy Weir","r":4.25,"d":"2026-03-31","p":496,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/18915521/","v":"I liked it! Always nice to learn some science ambiently through a space adventure. \n\nThe bromance levels are also unmatched, so glad I went into this not knowing anything hahah","c":["#102e33","#536559","#01232b"],"ar":0.649},{"id":35305,"t":"Sword in the Storm","a":"David Gemmell","r":4.5,"d":"2026-03-22","p":448,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/10712134/","v":"Favorite Gemmell book so far! Can’t wait to keep going. Brutal as always but the characters are a lot deeper than previous books.","c":["#4b3635","#69524c","#2a2019"],"ar":0.616},{"id":33031,"t":"The Goal","a":"Eliyahu M. Goldratt","r":4.75,"d":"2026-03-14","p":384,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19117072/","s":"A Process of Ongoing Improvement","v":"Phenomenal book, so relevant to building software in 2026, or just being better at thinking in general. Changed how I look at the world. I think I always have instinctively looked for bottlenecks, but never had good language for it, and definitely didn’t understand a lot of the counterintuitive lessons.\n\nFor example: optimizing locally in a system (with no other change) will often decrease the productivity of the overall system!\n\nI also love the form this was written in! A “business fable” that teaches nonfiction lessons in the fiction format (like a real novel… including the protagonist’s marital issues 😂)\n\nOnly improvement I can think of is to just go deeper into the ideas! Felt like it ended too soon.","c":["#a7b2b9","#648198","#6f595c"],"ar":0.652},{"id":33040,"t":"Quest for Lost Heroes","a":"David Gemmell","r":4.25,"d":"2026-03-06","p":304,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1773120/","v":"you need to be reading heroic fantasy novels from the 80s. you need to be studying fortress warfare. you need to be experiencing every protagonist you love dying brutally in the name of honor.","c":["#2f4435","#d5735f","#777976"],"ar":0.601},{"id":32385,"t":"Waylander","a":"David A. Gemmell","r":4,"d":"2026-02-26","p":384,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/22892863/","v":"Book 3 in the Drenai series down. This series, despite each book taking place in a new era with all new characters, has plots that feel strangely consistent.. \n\nDespite the consistency and almooost predictability, the characters are fresh and I just love a good fortress battle (a staple of the series).\n\nI still want to keep reading! Book 4 here i come…","c":["#271810","#e1f0b4","#ba6441"],"ar":0.611},{"id":32287,"t":"The King Beyond the Gate","a":"David Gemmell","r":4,"d":"2026-02-16","p":320,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1454365/","v":"Fun like the last book in the series and a pretty breezy read, but I wish it got more into the characters’ heads. The protagonist was basically inscrutable.\n\nStill going to read on..","c":["#402355","#ba4b3a","#c5815d"],"ar":0.604},{"id":2456,"t":"The Cold Start Problem","a":"Andrew Chen","r":null,"d":"2025-09-23","p":304,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16431849/","c":["#111bc8","#060837","#0b117f"],"ar":0.662},{"id":3774,"t":"Isles of the Emberdark","a":"Brandon Sanderson","r":4.25,"d":"2025-09-15","p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19793744/","c":["#070707","#302f2e","#1e1d1a"],"ar":0.643},{"id":20786,"t":"The Everything Store","a":"Brad Stone","r":3.75,"d":"2018-12-17","p":384,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16346598/","c":["#cbaa81","#ffffff","#988069"],"ar":0.645},{"id":20792,"t":"The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy","a":"Douglas Adams","r":null,"d":"2018-12-12","p":304,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/22190722/","c":["#221948","#352746","#7f7e6c"],"ar":0.689},{"id":71402,"t":"Deep Work","a":"Cal Newport","r":null,"d":"2017-05-22","p":304,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/18547431/","c":["#dfa72e","#fbcf4e","#282627"],"ar":0.662},{"id":20830,"t":"Payments Systems in the U.S.","a":"Carol Coye Benson, Scott Loftesness","r":null,"d":"2016-01-27","p":202,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1182969/","c":["#ffffff","#cbc6bc","#998a84"],"ar":0.768},{"id":887537,"t":"Shoe Dog","a":"Phil Knight","r":null,"d":null,"p":386,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1197445/","s":"A Memoir by the Creator of Nike","c":["#fe4332","#a12219","#f53a2a"],"ar":0.662},{"id":887536,"t":"Dealers of Lightning","a":"Michael A. Hiltzik","r":null,"d":null,"p":448,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/16433954/","c":["#fafbf6","#efeee3","#f7f7f3"],"ar":0.667},{"id":887535,"t":"Sapiens","a":"Yuval Noah Harari","r":null,"d":null,"p":456,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/18211293/","s":"A Brief History of Humankind","c":["#fffbfa","#dcbeb6","#fdfbfa"],"ar":0.652},{"id":887534,"t":"The Score Takes Care of Itself","a":"Steve Jamison, Bill Walsh, Craig Walsh","r":null,"d":null,"p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19098586/","s":"My Philosophy of Leadership","c":["#181315","#ced0ca","#b9b7ae"],"ar":0.692},{"id":887531,"t":"The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F*ck","a":"Mark Manson","r":null,"d":null,"p":224,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/6583861/","s":"A Counterintuitive Approach to Living a Good Life","c":["#fc4f01","#4b1f12","#d6480f"],"ar":0.669},{"id":887529,"t":"Masters of Doom","a":"David Kushner","r":null,"d":null,"p":336,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/2005817/","s":"How Two Guys Created an Empire and Transformed Pop Culture","c":["#4f2b12","#768131","#da130c"],"ar":0.648},{"id":67762,"t":"Do the Work","a":"Steven Pressfield","r":3,"d":null,"p":null,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/21920479/"},{"id":67750,"t":"Elon Musk","a":"Ashlee Vance","r":null,"d":null,"p":400,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/17792128/","c":["#090a0b","#221919","#a58374"],"ar":0.662},{"id":67747,"t":"The Black Swan","a":"Nassim Nicholas Taleb","r":4,"d":null,"p":480,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/21971501/","c":["#ffffff","#c31b37","#f7f6f6"],"ar":0.648},{"id":67742,"t":"Snow Crash","a":"Neal Stephenson","r":5,"d":null,"p":576,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19562198/","c":["#010107","#0a0921","#2c2852"],"ar":0.668},{"id":67741,"t":"The Moon Is a Harsh Mistress","a":"Robert A. Heinlein","r":5,"d":null,"p":288,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/18980937/","c":["#3d2122","#301819","#573031"],"ar":0.576},{"id":67740,"t":"Ego Is the Enemy","a":"Ryan Holiday","r":4,"d":null,"p":256,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/1997746/","c":["#094767","#012f4e","#03405f"],"ar":0.707},{"id":67734,"t":"Antifragile","a":"Nassim Nicholas Taleb","r":5,"d":null,"p":544,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/19396295/","c":["#ffffff","#a63c38","#c0a48a"],"ar":0.659},{"id":67733,"t":"Vagabonding","a":"Rolf Potts","r":null,"d":null,"p":240,"u":"https://readwise.io/books/2006917/","c":["#eed2bb","#5a4244","#699cb2"],"ar":0.654},{"id":67731,"t":"Art of Doing Science and Engi